    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lift is the upward force that opposes the weight of an aircraft and allows it to fly. According to Bernoulli's principle, air density decreases as airspeed increases, resulting in a region of lower air pressure above the wing and a region of higher air pressure below it. This pressure difference creates an upward force, or lift, on the wing when air flows over its curved surface. At the same time, Newton's third law, which states that every action has an equal and opposite reaction, comes into play, as the wing's upward force lifts the air downward, creating a continuous flow that generates additional lift, in effect creating a self-sustaining cycle of air movement.",
